%N A292846 Numbers k such that 11 iterations of 'Reverse and Subtract' lead to k, whereas fewer than 11 iterations do not lead to k.
%C A292846 There are 11 forty-two-digit terms in the sequence. Further terms are obtained (a) by inserting at the center of these terms either any number of 0's (for 166425621223026859056339052269787863565428, 311906350108036145286307567270199935391877, 466287189883036620417374974360601118217236, 658139747564935391877311906350534262959233, 703288139752915027377325180481642968027593) or any number of 9's (for the other six terms) and (b) by concatenating a term any number of times with itself and inserting an equal number of 0's at all junctures. Method (b) may be applied recursively to all terms. - _Ray Chandler_, Oct 14 2017
%C A292846 There are 22 forty-two-digit terms, forming two 11-cycles; the second cycle, containing 166425622146930945853551459969269763565428, was missing. In this second cycle too, five terms admit the insertion of any number of 0's at the center and the six others the insertion of any number of 9's. Completeness at 42 digits was checked by an exhaustive enumeration with an outside-in digit-pair automaton. - _Frédéric Fichepain_, Oct 10 2026
%H A292846 Frédéric Fichepain, <a href="/A292846/b292846.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..22</a> (correcting and extending 1..11 from Ray Chandler)
%H A292846 J. H. E. Cohn, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/28-2/cohn.pdf">Palindromic differences</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 28 (1990), no. 2, 113-120.
%F A292846 n = f^11(n), n <> f^k(n) for k < 11, where f: x -> |x - reverse(x)|.
